Subject: RE: Inquiry re Museums Victoria specimen Mamenchisaurus in exhibition

Hi Mike,

I had time to look through our annual reports this morning and I have some relevant information for you, but not necessarily a confirmation that 'your' Mamenchisaurus specimen was exhibited I'm sorry.

In the Annual Report 1987-1988, we definitely had a bit of a bumper year for dinosaurs, and this exhibition matches the one you described, that was developed by Museum Victoria and toured.

'Dinosaurs 29/2/88 to 31/7/88

An exhibition of dinosaurs and animals that lived during the reign of the dinosaurs. Organised in conjunction with Museum Victoria.

''Dinosaurs

One of the most successful of all Museum exhibitions, the planning for Dinosaurs commenced two years ago. In early 1987 a number of specimens from the museum's collection were made available to the Museum of Victoria for their exhibition Esso Return of the Dinosaurs. In addition an articulated skeleton and life size model of the dinosaur Hypsilophodon were produced for this exhibition in Melbourne.

The exhibition was presented in Auckland New Zealand before coming to Launceston. A considerable number of specimens and reconstructions were added to the already impressive exhibition. Amongst the additions were life size reconstructions of the flying reptile Pteranodon with a fire metre wingspan, the dinosaur Coelophysis and a pair of ichthyosaurs.

Supporting the exhibition were a number of special programmes. The planetarium show Nemesis: The End of the Dinosaurs dealt with possible causes of the dinosaurs extinction…. Within the exhibition itself, the articulation and erection of the skeletons of the dinosaurs Muttaburrasaurus and Deinonychus was undertaken. The latter skeleton was being shown for the first time in Australia and was articulated by Robert Allen from the Peabody Museum, Yalew, New Haven in Connecticut, United States of America.'

'More than 58,106 people visited the exhibition of which 10,000 were school children from throughout the state and the Bass Strait islands.'

At the same time, a travelling exhibition was mentioned as in development:

'Australian Vertebrate Fossils - Western Australian Museum, Perth, Los Angeles County Natural History Museum, Los Angeles United States of America'

I've scanned in two spread pages from this report as it includes two photographs. One is of articulated dinosaur skeletons, including what I suspect is a Tsintaosaurus Dinosaur Walk | Resources | Melbourne Museum

Because both Tsintaosaurus and Mamenchisaurus were dinosaurs discovered in China, and both skeletons are grouped in the Museums Victoria collection above, it's certainly possible that the Mamenchisaurus skeleton could have been shown at Royal Park, Queen Victoria Museum and Art Gallery. At 25 metres long, I think it could just have fit, but would have had to have been articulated onsite. I wonder if it may not have come because of its size - but can't confirm that yet. I am a little suspicious, because building such a large and impressive dinosaur onsite would have been something I'd think the annual report would have mentioned, especially since it mentions several others.

Please let me know if you would like us to look into this further through our archives, but this is a summary of the information available in the Annual Report.

